[[:db/cas 17592186060935 :tutorial/content "Remember that we can represent a system of two linear equations in two variables using matrices. We can do the same thing for a system of three linear equations in three variables, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y\\text{ }-\\text{ }2z &=7 \\\\ 2x\\text{ }-\\text{ }1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }4z &=2 \\\\ 2x+1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }3z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor a $3 \\times 3$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $3 \\times 3$ coefficient matrix:\n\n\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $3 \\times 1$ column vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}7} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{x} = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}7} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can write any system of three equations with three variables in this form. Just to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3x+2y\\text{ }-\\text{ }2z \\\\ 2x\\text{ }-\\text{ }1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }4z \\\\ 2x+1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }3z\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =b\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that we can represent a system of two linear equations in two variables using matrices. We can do the same thing for a system of three linear equations in three variables, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{3}}x+{\\color{blue}{2}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{2}}z &={\\color{red}{7}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{4}}z &={\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{3}}z &={\\color{red}{3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor a $3 \\times 3$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $3 \\times 3$ coefficient matrix:\n\n\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $3 \\times 1$ column vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}7} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{x} = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}7} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can write any system of three equations with three variables in this form. Just to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{-2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}} & {\\color{blue}{-4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{-3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}}x+{\\color{blue}{2}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{2}}z \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{4}}z \\\\ {\\color{blue}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}}y\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{-}}\\text{ }{\\color{blue}{3}}z\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{7}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186116497 :tutorial/content "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n$$\nE = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nEA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6} \\\\ {\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n$$\nE = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nEA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6} \\\\ {\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{8}} & {\\color{blue}{6}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{8}} & {\\color{blue}{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{4}} & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{6}} & {\\color{red}{8}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121024 :tutorial/content "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\,\\,=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em}=\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+\\det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & … & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{4}}+{\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n}+{{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{red}{◻}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {{\\color{blue}{△}}}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-2} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{4}+\\underbrace{det\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{0}} & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265857 :question/worked-solution "Here, $S$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $T$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $S$ and $2$ rows in $T$, so the product is undefined:\n\n$$\n{S}_{3\\times 1}{\\times }_{\\ne }{T}_{2\\times 1}=\\text{undefined}\n$$" "Here, $S$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $T$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $S$ and $2$ rows in $T$, so the product is undefined:\n\n$$\n{S}_{3\\times {\\color{red}{1}}}{\\times }_{{\\color{red}{\\ne }}}{T}_{{\\color{red}{2}}\\times 1}={\\color{red}{\\text{undefined}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265950 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160+0 & 184+0 & 76+2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160+0 & 184+0 & 76+2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 76\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 76\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0+2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (160\\cdot 4-184\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =176\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{184}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{76}}+{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{184}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{76}}+{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{160}} & {\\color{red}{184}} & {\\color{red}{76}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}160 & 184 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (160\\cdot 4-184\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =176\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265974 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{vmatrix}}_{8}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8+(-6+6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{(b+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{red}{(b+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b+2}}\\end{vmatrix}}_{8}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{-2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8+(-6+6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186266066 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{34}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 & 4 & {\\color{red}{-6}} \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & {\\color{red}{-6}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{34}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186266127 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 10 & -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{-6}} & {\\color{red}{9}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 10 & -4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186354636 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{4}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} & {\\color{red}{c}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{4}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354637 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{5}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{(b+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+2)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{(b+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{3}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+2)}} & -2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{(b+3)}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{5}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354639 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{92}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{93}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{92}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{93}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{90}} & {\\color{red}{92}} & {\\color{red}{93}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354640 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{98}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{98}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{96}} & {\\color{red}{98}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357503 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & {\\color{red}{4}} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & {\\color{blue}{-2}} & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & {\\color{red}{8}} & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & {\\color{red}{2}} & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186357508 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-4}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186357512 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-2}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} & {\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186357513 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{4}} & {\\color{red}{-2}} & {\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186119593 :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$" "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119594 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}_{-10}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{c}}+{\\color{blue}{1}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} & {\\color{red}{c}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}}_{-10}+\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388251 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -11 & 7 & 9 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -4 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-2}} \\\\ -11 & 7 & 9 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186392528 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {\\color{red}{-2}} & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{blue}{1}} & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{red}{4}} & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & {\\color{red}{-1}} & -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186394840 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & {\\color{blue}{-1}} & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{red}{3}} & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{red}{-1}} & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & {\\color{red}{2}} & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186394854 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{42}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-3}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{42}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186119690 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{1}} & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ {\\color{red}{4}} & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-3}} & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186119691 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186120435 :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{vmatrix}}_{-7}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{4}} & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{(a+3)}}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}}+{\\color{blue}{4}} & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{a+3}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}} & 6\\end{vmatrix}}_{-7}+\\begin{vmatrix}-{\\color{blue}{1}} & -5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4}} & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120457 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{215}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{215}}+{\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{99}}+{\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}} \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{red}{107}} & {\\color{red}{215}} & {\\color{red}{99}}\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120545 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-2}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{-6}} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186120546 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & {\\color{red}{12}} \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & {\\color{red}{-6}} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & {\\color{red}{-3}}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186120547 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{red}{6}} & {\\color{red}{-4}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\,7\\,)}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{11}}-\\underbrace{(\\,1\\,)}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{12}}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{13}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{7}} & {\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{7}}\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{11}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{11}}}}-\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{1\\hspace{0.167em}}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{12}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{12}}}}+\\underbrace{({\\color{red}{-3}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{13}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{13}}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!"] [:db/cas 17592186093403 :tutorial/content "We know that we can write a system of three linear equations in three variables in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n- $A$ is the $3\\times 3$ coefficient matrix,\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}$ is the column vector of variables, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}$ is the column vector of constants.\n\nIf the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ by \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{3}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see how this works for a particular system in the next example." "We know that we can write a system of three linear equations in three variables in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n- $A$ is the $3\\times 3$ coefficient matrix,\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}$ is the column vector of variables, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}$ is the column vector of constants.\n\nIf the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ by \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {I}_{3}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see how this works for a particular system in the next example."] [:db/cas 17592186107082 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ 2x-y-z &=-1 \\\\ 2y-6z &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z &=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ y-3z &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3+2z \\\\ y &=-5+3z \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}= \\begin{bmatrix}-3+2z \\\\ -5+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\n\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$" "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ 2x-y-z &=-1 \\\\ 2y-6z &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z &=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=2 \\\\ y-3z &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3+2z \\\\ y &=-5+3z \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}= \\begin{bmatrix}-3+2z \\\\ -5+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\n\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186121022 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will consider a few more properties of determinants. Let's start with the following important property. \n\n*The determinant of the product of two square matrices is equal to the product of the determinants.*\n\nSymbolically, if $M$ and $N$ are both square matrices, then\n\n$$\n\\det  ( M \\cdot  N ) = \\det  ( M ) \\cdot  \\det  ( N ) .\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to find the determinant of the matrix $A$ that is defined as follows:\n$$\nA = \n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 2 & - 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}3 & - 10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo easily compute $\\det(A)$, we first compute the determinants of the two matrices and then multiply the results. Also, notice that both matrices in our product are triangular, so their determinants are given by the product of the diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\det  ( A ) &  = \\det  \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 2 & - 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] \\cdot  \\det  \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}3 & - 10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & - 1\\end{array} \\right] \\\\  &  = \\underset{\\text{lower triangular}}{\\underbrace{∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}1} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ - 2 & {\\color{red}- 3} & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ 2 & 0 & {\\color{red}2}\\end{array} ∣}} \\cdot  \\underset{\\text{upper triangular}}{\\underbrace{∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}3} & - 10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}5} & 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}- 1}\\end{array} ∣}} \\\\  &  = ( {\\color{red}1} \\cdot  ( {\\color{red}- 3} ) \\cdot  {\\color{red}2} ) \\cdot  ( {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  {\\color{red}5} \\cdot  ( {\\color{red}- 1} ) ) \\\\  &  = ( - 6 ) \\cdot  ( - 15 ) \\\\  &  = 90\\end{array}\n$$" "In this topic, we will consider a few more properties of determinants. Let's start with the following important property. \n\n*The determinant of the product of two square matrices is equal to the product of the determinants.*\n\nSymbolically, if $M$ and $N$ are both square matrices, then\n\n$$\n\\det  ( M \\cdot  N ) = \\det  ( M ) \\cdot  \\det  ( N ) .\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to find the determinant of the matrix $A$ that is defined as follows:\n$$\nA = \n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - 2 & - 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}3 & - 10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo easily compute $\\det(A)$, we first compute the determinants of the two matrices and then multiply the results. Also, notice that both matrices in our product are triangular, so their determinants are given by the product of the diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) & =\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ -2 & {\\color{red}{-3}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 2 & 0 & {\\color{red}{2}}\\end{vmatrix}}_{\\text{lower triangular}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{3}} & -10 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{5}} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{red}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}_{\\text{upper triangular}} \\\\  & =({\\color{red}{1}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}})\\cdot ({\\color{red}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{5}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-1}})) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-15) \\\\  & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121046 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a determinant calculation is to create a row (or column) containing all zeros except for one entry. Then, we can use a Laplace expansion across that row (or column) to compute the determinant.\n\nLet's demonstrate this method on the matrix $A$ below.\n\n$$\nA=\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & 1 & {\\color{black}1} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {\\color{black}3} \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can make entries to the right of $a_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary *column* operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one column to another column in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(21-10) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "One way to simplify a determinant calculation is to create a row (or column) containing all zeros except for one entry. Then, we can use a Laplace expansion across that row (or column) to compute the determinant.\n\nLet's demonstrate this method on the matrix $A$ below.\n\n$$\nA=\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & 1 & {\\color{black}1} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {\\color{black}3} \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can make entries to the right of $a_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary *column* operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one column to another column in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.167em}\\phantom{=}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & 1 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & 14 & 17\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\hspace{2em}\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & -3 & -5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.167em}\\phantom{=}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & -3 & -5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1}} & {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{red}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{8}} & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{1}}\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(21-10) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^0:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\textrm{no basis}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^1:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^2:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}\n\t\\Bigg\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^3:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{k}}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0}) &=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\cdots, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^0:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\textrm{no basis}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^1:\n\t\\big\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}\n\t\\big\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^2:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}\n\t\\Bigg\\},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbb{R}^3:\n\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{i}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{j}}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{k}}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{0}) &=0,\\hspace{1em}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{1})=1,\\hspace{1em}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{2})=2,\\hspace{1em}\\text{dim}({\\mathbb{R}}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55